menu "espix filesystem"

    config ESPIX_FS_EXFAT
        bool "exFAT support in the FAT driver"
        default n
        help
            FatFs reads and writes exFAT as well as FAT12/16/32, but ESP-IDF
            ships it off: ffconf.h hard-codes FF_FS_EXFAT to 0 and exposes no
            Kconfig for it, unlike the options beside it. Enabling this patches
            that line to follow this symbol, so it is a build choice rather
            than an edit to the IDF tree.

            It also turns on FF_LBA64, which is not optional for the volumes
            exFAT is wanted for: ff.c refuses any volume whose last LBA does not
            fit in 32 bits, which at a 512-byte sector is 2TiB. Without it an
            exFAT partition larger than that mounts as FR_NO_FILESYSTEM.

            Two more 32-bit fields had to widen with it, both in the IDF tree
            and both silent when left alone: the diskio read/write function
            pointers in diskio_impl.h, and the sector parameters and ioctl
            buffers in diskio_bdl.c. See tools/patch-fatfs.py.

            With it on, `mount` accepts a volume lsblk names exfat and lsblk
            stops marking that type unsupported -- the driver is there, so the
            marker would be a lie.

            The cost, measured on the S3 build with idf.py size -- this option n
            against the same tree with it y, so the delta is the whole feature
            rather than one patch of it:

              total image   1347280 -> 1354252    +6972 bytes
              .bss            59528 -> 59528      no change
              .data           26503 -> 26503      no change

            The earlier figure for FF_FS_EXFAT alone was +5644; the rest is
            FF_LBA64. Nothing shows in .bss because a FATFS work area is
            allocated rather than static, so the RAM cost sits on the heap at
            mount. Expect a 3TB volume to allocate a few KB more there and to
            spend a moment reading its allocation bitmap the first time free
            space is asked for.

            Licensing: exFAT is covered by Microsoft patents, and FatFs's author
            has historically noted that a licence may be needed for commercial
            use. No patent or licence text ships with IDF's FatFs, and that claim
            has not been verified here -- check the terms before shipping this on
            by default.

endmenu
